  "references": "**Patient.** 78-year-old man admitted with choledocholithiasis. Two days of RUQ/epigastric pain, more prolonged than his prior self-limited colicky episodes, with nausea, dark urine and lighter stools; scleral icterus noticed by his daughter. No prior abdominal surgery. Exam: vital signs normal, scleral icterus, mild RUQ tenderness, negative Murphy’s sign, no peritonitis, oriented. Ultrasound: cholelithiasis; CBD dilated to 9 mm; echogenic focus with acoustic shadowing in the distal CBD; no gallbladder wall thickening or pericholecystic fluid.\n\n**Supporting labs (case background; not given to the learner).**\n\n|  | Admission (48 h ago) | Repeat, just before sign-out |\n|---|---|---|\n| Total bilirubin (0.2 to 1.2 mg/dL) | 4.8 | 4.6 |\n| Direct bilirubin (0 to 0.3 mg/dL) | 3.6 | 3.5 |\n| Alkaline phosphatase (40 to 130 U/L) | 275 | 268 |\n| GGT (8 to 61 U/L) | 450 | 442 |\n| ALT (7 to 56 U/L) | 168 | 160 |\n| AST (10 to 40 U/L) | 152 | 141 |\n| Lipase (13 to 60 U/L) | 41 | 38 |\n| WBC (4.5 to 11 x10^3/uL) | 8.4 | 7.9 |\n\nVital signs: T 37.0 C, HR 78, BP 138/76, RR 16, SpO2 97% on room air. Afebrile throughout; hemodynamically stable; pain well controlled.\n\n**Key clinical facts.**\n\n1. Mr. Miller meets ASGE high-probability predictors (CBD stone on ultrasound; total bilirubin >4 mg/dL with CBD dilated >6 mm); the recommended strategy is to proceed to ERCP. [ASGE 2019, Table 15]\n2. Urgent ERCP (within 48 hours) benefits patients with cholangitis; the guideline sets no time window for choledocholithiasis without cholangitis. [ASGE 2019]\n3. He does not have cholangitis or pancreatitis: afebrile, normal WBC, hemodynamically stable, normal lipase, stable cholestatic labs. [Case data]\n4. While he waits, the team monitors him for signs of infection. [Case data]\n5. Premature reassurance and blocking are communication behaviors to avoid. [Back 2005, p. 165]\n\n**References.**\n\n1. Buxbaum JL, Abbas Fehmi SM, Sultan S, et al. ASGE guideline on the role of endoscopy in the evaluation and management of choledocholithiasis. *Gastrointest Endosc.* 2019;89(6):1075-1105.e15. doi:10.1016/j.gie.2018.10.001\n2. Back AL, Arnold RM, Baile WF, Tulsky JA, Fryer-Edwards K. Approaching difficult communication tasks in oncology. *CA Cancer J Clin.* 2005;55(3):164-177. doi:10.3322/canjclin.55.3.164",
